<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Axayacatl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1469">1469</a>–<a href="/years/1481">1481</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Axayacatl was the sixth tlatoani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, ruling in the later fifteenth century. He conquered the neighbouring city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> in 1473 and led campaigns in the Toluca valley. His attempted invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> kingdom in 1479 ended in a severe defeat for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/axayacatl.jpg" alt="Axayacatl" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Axayacatl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Axayacatl, whose name means face of water in Classical Nahuatl, was born around 1449. He was a son of the princess Atotoztli II and her cousin, the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a>, and a grandson of the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a>. His brothers were the later rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tizoc">Tizoc</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a>, and he was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a> and Cuitláhuac and an uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a>. In his youth his military ability won him the favour of influential figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a> and Tlacaelel I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a> in 1469 Axayacatl was chosen to take the throne, to the displeasure of his two older brothers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tizoc">Tizoc</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a>. He ruled for twelve years and spent most of them consolidating his reputation as a warrior. The Great Sun Stone, also known as the Aztec Calendar, was carved under his leadership, and an earthquake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> destroyed many homes during his reign.</p><p>In 1473, using the insulting behaviour of a few citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> as a pretext, he invaded the neighbouring city, killed its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moquihuix">Moquihuix</a> and replaced him with a military governor. The Tlatelolcans lost any voice they had in forming Aztec policy. In 1474 he led a successful campaign against the Matlatzinca of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toluca-valley">Toluca Valley</a>.</p><p>He was then defeated by the Tarascans of Michoacan, the only major defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> had suffered up to that time. Despite some minor later successes, the defeat permanently damaged his image.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Although still young, Axayacatl fell gravely ill in 1480 and died a year later, in 1481. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tizoc">Tizoc</a> succeeded him. Axayacatl is also remembered as a poet who wrote two poems. The first, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Axayacatl, is a defence against his brothers and critics. The second, the Song of the Ancients, is a lament written after his defeat in Michoacan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Axayacatl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army's clearest limitation was exposed by the Purépecha (Tarascan) state in the Aztec–Purépecha war of 1479, when <b>Axayacatl</b>'s force, reportedly 24,000 strong, was routed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 1470s (sources date the campaign between 1476 and 1479) the Aztec ruler <b>Axayacatl</b> led a large army into Michoacán and was routed near Taximaroa, now Ciudad Hidalgo; the chronicler Diego Durán recorded that some 20,000 of his men died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1473 <b>Axayacatl</b> of Tenochtitlan defeated Tlatelolco and killed its ruler Moquihuix, and the city was annexed by the Aztec Empire under a military governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1475" aria-label="Tenochtitlan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Axayacatl"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4267 5869h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4267 5869h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1475">What was here in 1475? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
